{
  "id": "u11",
  "title": "One or Many",
  "subtitle": "Id-dar, l-affarijiet u l-annimali: making more than one",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "flat",
      "icon": "🔑",
      "title": "The new flat · a viewing",
      "subtitle": "Il-flett il-ġdid: counting what is in a place",
      "intro": "Joanne is seeing a flat and Karl is showing her round. Almost every answer is a plural. Listen for how the words change when there is more than one.",
      "dialogue": [
        {
          "mt": "Kemm hemm kmamar?",
          "en": "How many rooms are there?"
        },
        {
          "mt": "Hemm żewġ kmamar tas-sodda u salott kbir.",
          "en": "There are two bedrooms and a big living room."
        },
        {
          "mt": "U l-kċina?",
          "en": "And the kitchen?"
        },
        {
          "mt": "Fil-kċina hemm żewġ twieqi kbar.",
          "en": "In the kitchen there are two big windows."
        },
        {
          "mt": "Hemm garaxx?",
          "en": "Is there a garage?"
        },
        {
          "mt": "Le, imma hemm bejt u terazzin żgħir.",
          "en": "No, but there is a roof and a small terrace."
        },
        {
          "mt": "U l-għamara?",
          "en": "And the furniture?"
        },
        {
          "mt": "Hemm sodda, mejda u tliet siġġijiet.",
          "en": "There is a bed, a table and three chairs."
        },
        {
          "mt": "Nieħdu ċ-ċwievet illum?",
          "en": "Do we take the keys today?"
        },
        {
          "mt": "Iva, dawn iċ-ċwievet.",
          "en": "Yes, these are the keys."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "house",
      "icon": "🏠",
      "title": "Around the house",
      "subtitle": "Id-dar: the words on every Maltese property advert",
      "intro": "Types of home, and the parts of one. Tap a card to flip it: the front is one, the back is more than one.",
      "facts": [
        "You already met dar, kċina and the rooms. Here each one gains its plural.",
        "Kamra means room, so the room words are all built the same way: kamra tas-sodda, kamra tal-banju, kamra tal-ikel. Make kamra plural and the whole phrase follows: kmamar tas-sodda."
      ],
