    {
      "id": "plural11",
      "icon": "🔢",
      "title": "Grammar",
      "subtitle": "Il-plural: making more than one",
      "intro": "Maltese has two ways of making a plural, and then one rule about counting that catches everybody out.",
      "facts": [
        "There is no single plural ending in Maltese. Which route a word takes is part of the word.",
        "The second route is the same machinery as the past tense you met last unit: the consonants hold still and the vowels move around them."
      ],
      "rules": [
        {
          "id": "pl-ending",
          "title": "Route one: the word keeps its shape and takes an ending",
          "explanation": "The word stays recognisable and an ending is added, usually -i, -jiet or -s. Words Maltese borrowed from English or Italian nearly always go this way, so if a word looks familiar to you, this is the likely route.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "mt": "siġġu",
              "en": "chair, siġġijiet"
            },
            {
              "mt": "garaxx",
              "en": "garage, garaxxijiet"
            },
            {
              "mt": "salott",
              "en": "living room, salotti"
            },
            {
              "mt": "pixxina",
              "en": "swimming pool, pixxini"
            },
            {
              "mt": "kabord",
              "en": "cupboard, kabords"
            },
            {
              "mt": "lift",
              "en": "lift, liftijiet"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pl-broken",
          "title": "Route two: the word is rebuilt from the inside",
          "explanation": "Here the ending is not the point. The consonants stay in order and the vowels between them change, so the plural can look like a different word. This is the older Maltese layer, and it is the same pattern that turns kiteb into ktibt in the past tense.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "mt": "kelb",
              "en": "dog, klieb"
            },
            {
              "mt": "dar",
              "en": "house, djar"
            },
            {
              "mt": "forn",
              "en": "oven, fran"
            },
            {
              "mt": "tieqa",
              "en": "window, twieqi"
            },
            {
              "mt": "kamra",
              "en": "room, kmamar"
            },
            {
              "mt": "qattus",
              "en": "cat, qtates"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pl-counting",
          "title": "Counting: and then it goes back to one",
          "explanation": "Two takes żewġ with the plural. Three to ten take the plural too. But from eleven upwards the noun goes back to the SINGULAR, which feels wrong in English and is the mistake learners make most.",
          "letters": [
            "żewġ + plural, for two",
            "three to ten + plural",
            "eleven and above + singular"
          ],
          "examples": [
            {
              "mt": "żewġ siġġijiet",
              "en": "two chairs"
            },
            {
              "mt": "tliet twieqi",
              "en": "three windows"
            },
            {
              "mt": "ħames kmamar",
              "en": "five rooms"
            },
            {
              "mt": "għaxar bibien",
              "en": "ten doors"
            },
            {
              "mt": "ħdax-il siġġu",
              "en": "eleven chairs"
            },
            {
              "mt": "għoxrin tieqa",
              "en": "twenty windows"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "exercises": [
        {
